// `keys(m) -> [K]` over both Map worlds, added 2026-10-22.
//
// **Why it is not an overload of `keys`.** `keys(m) -> Set<K>` and `keys(m) -> [K]` differ only in
// what they answer, and two declarations may share a name only when every *call* can tell them
// apart -- which a return type cannot do. Checked directly: two `fn f(x: int)` differing only in
// return type are refused with "function 'f' is already defined ... two declarations may share a
// name only when every call can tell them apart". `math.floor`/`math.floor_float` is the
// established answer to this shape and `keys` follows it.
//
// **Why it is not `to_array(m)`.** That name would claim to convert the map, where a map answers
// three different questions -- its keys, its values, its entries -- and `to_array` could only be
// one of them. `to_array` is for a collection with a single obvious element type, which is why it
// takes the two set worlds.
//
// **Why it is not just `keys(m)`.** That builds an entire `Set<K>` -- every key hashed
// into a trie -- and then walks it back out into the array that was wanted. Measured at about 10x
// this on a 100,000-entry map, which is the fused-conversion rule DESIGN.md states being satisfied
// by a wide margin rather than by a judgement call.
//
// The immutable member shares one C function with `to_array`'s Set member: a Set is a MapObj whose
// values are placeholders, so a set's elements and a map's keys are the same walk.
fn main() {
    val mb = mutable_map_new<string, int>()
    set(mb, "b", 2)
    set(mb, "a", 1)
    set(mb, "c", 3)
    val m = snapshot(mb)

    // insertion order, and the same order `values` uses -- the correspondence is the point
    print(keys(m))
    print(values(m))
    print(keys(m))

    // the builder world answers the same thing
    print(keys(mb))

    // an empty map in either world is an empty array, not an error
    val e = mutable_map_new<int, int>()
    print(keys(e))
    print(keys(snapshot(e)))

    // a packable key type, where the native's array_try_pack earns its keep
    val nb = mutable_map_new<int, string>()
    set(nb, 10, "x")
    set(nb, 20, "y")
    print(keys(snapshot(nb)))

    // named argument, which an ordinary declaration takes
    print(keys(m: m))
}
